<p><img src="ita-wegman-main.png" class="a" /></p>
<h1>The Artificial Anthroposophical Society – a continuation</h1>
<h2>by Frank Thomas Smith</h2>
<p class="intro">In <a
href="https://southerncrossreview.org/161/artificial-society.html"
class="uri">https://southerncrossreview.org/161/artificial-society.html</a>
<em>The Artificial Anthroposophical Society</em> I describe the current
situation of the problematic General Anthropsophical Society, without
recommending a possible solution. Why not? Because I knew of none. Now,
however, after some further study, certain concepts which I would like
to share with you have fallen into my lap, so to speak.</p>
<p>In February 1935 Ita Wegman wrote to Maria Röschl:</p>
<p>“All the old forms, also the very last one for Anthroposophy (the
Christmas Meeting Society 1923) have been basically broken, and it seems
to me now that one should no longer seek a form for the life of
Anthroposophy, but that every individual is the form with whom
Anthroposophy wants unite. Where this is the case, others will find it
and join to become members of the true spiritual association. The
Anthroposophical Society is no longer necessary because Anthroposophy is
already on the earth. It depends now on the individuals and they must
together form a higher association, by means of their own development,
that has its roots in the spiritual world…”</p>
<p>In March 1935 she wrote in a letter to Herbert Hahn:</p>
<p>“With the Christmas Meeting Dr. Steiner anticipated an organization
that was not possible to implement because the old forces did not yet
permit it … Perhaps a future form can be possible once some people free
themselves from the Society.”<br />
<sub>From Peter Selg “Geistiger Widerstand und Überwindung” Ita Wegman
1925-1933, Dornach 2005</sub></p>
<p>From Gerhard von Beckerath in his book “<em>Rudolf Steiners
Leidensweg” 2011</em>:</p>
<p>“Today as Rudolf Steiner’s student I must call to <em>all,</em> not
just a few of Rudolf Steiner’s students: Yes, it has now become certain
that in 1935 Ita Wegman correctly assessed what the possible form the
anthroposophical movement on earth should take today. In my opinion, we
must have the courage to truly perceive the realities that are becoming
ever more apparent and to act accordingly, so that not only a few but
all of Rudolf Steiner’s students sooner or later become free of the
Society – instead of continuing being driven!</p>
<p>Or do we, by the persistence of outdated but ingrained habits of
thought, continue to believe that Rudolf Steiner’s “shattered” social
experiment of 1923 with the Christmas Conference can still be practiced
by us today – even without Rudolf Steiner? ……. Have we not learned from
painful experience to recognize the best possible form for the
anthroposophical movement on earth to take today without Rudolf Steiner?
With truthfulness, honesty, modesty and patience individually achieved.
Instead of simply maintaining the current form of the Society after
Rudolf Steiner’s death, with its many demands and <em>centrally</em>
oriented structure, a social confederative organism <a href="#fn1"
class="footnote-ref" id="fnref1" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a>
could be formed on the basis of autonomous local associations of people
striving for anthroposophy. This would no longer demand more than what
we can achieve in the foreseeable future….The local associations could
then join together into Country-Associations. (Country Societies already
exist.) Together these would form a World-Association that has its
active center in the Goetheanum in Dornach, without attempting to
implement the Christmas Meeting structure. The various
Country-Associations would have the duty to maintain the Goetheanum,
which would continue to house the Free University for Spiritual
Science…”</p>
<p>I will now speak for myself: In English and in most other languages
beyond the Germanic ones, the name “Free School for Spiritual Science”
is used, although the original German reads Freie <em>Hochschule</em>
für Gesteswissenschaft – “Hochschule” means “University”. Perhaps we say
“School” because it would be embarrassing to call something a university
which is not one at all. A solution to this mis-translation would be to
make it correct: turn the <em>Hochschule</em> into a <em>real</em>
university, with a curriculum of subjects of study resulting in various
degrees: literary, scientific, professional, etc. and, of course,
spiritual science/Anthroposophy. Courses could be in various languages,
depending on the Professors’ ability and wishes, with emphasis on
English to make the university accessible to students of many
nationalities.</p>
<section id="footnotes" class="footnotes footnotes-end-of-document"
role="doc-endnotes">
<hr />
<ol>
<li id="fn1"><p>A confederation is a union of independent associations
that work together for a common purpose.<a href="#fnref1"
class="footnote-back" role="doc-backlink">↩︎</a></p></li>
</ol>
</section>
<p><em>Victorious spirit<br />
Consume the powerlessness<br />
Of timid souls.<br />
Burn away selfishness,<br />
Ignite compassion,<br />
So that selflessness<br />
May flow through humanity’s life<br />
As the source<br />
Of spiritual rebirth.</em></p>
<p><em>Sieghafter Geist<br />
Durchflamme die Ohnmacht<br />
Zaghafter Seelen.<br />
Verbrenne die Ichsucht,<br />
Entzünde das Mitleid,<br />
Dass Selbstlosigkeit<br />
Das Lebensstrom der Menschheit<br />
Wallt als Quelle<br />
Der gistigen Wiedergeburt.</em><br />
Rudolf Steiner (GA 268)</p>
